    strengthAxis: "detail",
    approximateScore: 89,
    excerpt: `I own forty-three pairs of shoes I will never wear, and each one taught me something about human desire that no textbook could. The Jordan 4 Retro "Bred Reimagined" — bought for retail at $200, sold for $410 to a man who drove forty minutes because he missed the drop — taught me scarcity. The New Balance 550s that sat in my closet for three months taught me that trends have half-lives. The counterfeit Yeezys I almost bought on a Discord server for $90 taught me due diligence, which is a polite way of saying I learned to check the stitching before I check the price.

I started selling on South Street at fifteen, laying pairs on a folding table next to a man named Gerald who sold incense and called me "young professor." Gerald taught me the first rule of street sales: people don't buy shoes, they buy the version of themselves they think the shoes will create. He was right. The man who bought the Breds didn't need them. He needed to be the kind of person who owned them. Understanding that distinction — between the object and the aspiration — was worth more than the $210 profit.

By junior year, I had reinvested $4,300 in profits into a micro-lending program at my school, giving $50 interest-free loans to classmates for SAT prep books, prom tickets, and one graphing calculator that Marcus swore he'd return but didn't. The sneakers were the business. The lending was the point.`,
