    excerpt: `My sister and I have fought over the television remote every evening for eleven years, and last summer I realized we had been playing a Nash equilibrium without knowing it. Here is the game: Priya wants to watch cooking shows. I want to watch documentaries. We each have one hour of screen time. If we both insist on our preference, my mother intervenes and assigns the Nature Channel, which neither of us wants. Over the years, without ever discussing it, we developed a rotation — she gets Monday, Wednesday, Friday; I get Tuesday, Thursday, Saturday; Sundays are negotiated in real time based on homework load and general goodwill.

I mapped this out on graph paper after reading about the Prisoner's Dilemma in a library book. Our rotation was, mathematically, the optimal cooperative strategy. We had arrived at it through eleven years of arguments, sulking, and one incident involving a thrown couch cushion, but we had arrived at it. When I showed Priya the diagram, she said, "You turned our fights into math?" and I said, "Our fights were always math. We just didn't have the notation."

That sentence — our fights were always math — became the thesis of my independent study. I spent the fall modeling household negotiations as iterated games: who does the dishes, who walks the dog, who gets the car on Friday night. Every family, I discovered, is an economy. Every sibling relationship is a repeated game with incomplete information, emotional side payments, and a regulator (Mom) whose utility function prioritizes silence above all else.`,
