    excerpt: `The 1998 Honda Civic my uncle gave me had 247,000 miles, no working air conditioning, and a cassette deck that only played the left channel — and it became the best education I ever received. Uncle Ray handed me the keys on my sixteenth birthday and said, "She runs. Everything else is your problem." He was not exaggerating. Within three weeks, the alternator died, the brake pads ground down to bare metal, and a noise emerged from the engine bay that my friend Kyle described as "a cat arguing with a garbage disposal."

I could not afford a mechanic. What I could afford was Wi-Fi. I found a YouTube channel called "ChrisFix" and watched a nineteen-minute video on alternator replacement seven times, pausing every thirty seconds to match what I saw on screen with what I saw under my hood. The first time I removed the serpentine belt, it took two hours. The second time — because I reinstalled it wrong and had to redo it — took forty minutes. The third time took twelve. This is, I later learned in physics class, called a learning curve, but under the hood of a Civic at 10 p.m., it just felt like stubbornness paying off.

By senior year, I had replaced the alternator, the brake pads, the radiator hose, and the passenger-side window motor. Total parts cost: $340. Equivalent mechanic labor: over $2,000. I kept a log of every repair in a composition notebook — part name, cost, time spent, mistakes made. That notebook is, in its own greasy way, an engineering journal. Each entry is a problem set that fought back.`,
