    strengthAxis: "place",
    approximateScore: 89,
    excerpt: `I teach algebra in Mandarin on Saturday mornings to a room of grandmothers who are better at arithmetic than I am and worse at word problems because the words are in English. Mrs. Chen can multiply three-digit numbers in her head faster than I can type them into a calculator. She ran a fabric shop in Guangzhou for thirty years. She is not bad at math. She is bad at a specific kind of math that requires reading a paragraph in a language she is still learning and extracting the equation hidden inside the story.

This distinction — between mathematical ability and mathematical access — changed how I think about education. The GED prep book I was assigned to use at the Chinatown Community Center was written for native English speakers. Every word problem assumed cultural fluency: "If a contractor charges $45 per hour and the job takes 6.5 hours, what is the total cost before tax?" Mrs. Chen can do the multiplication. She doesn't know what a contractor is. The math is not the barrier. The context is.

I started rewriting the problems. "If a fabric merchant buys 20 meters of silk at 35 yuan per meter and sells it for 52 yuan per meter, what is the profit?" Mrs. Chen solved it in eight seconds. She looked at me and said, in Mandarin, "Why didn't they just ask it this way?" I didn't have a good answer, which is how I realized that the question itself was the problem.`,
