    strengthAxis: "surprise",
    approximateScore: 90,
    excerpt: `Maya can't relevé, but she can spin a wheelchair 720 degrees with the precision of a figure skater, and our recital piece used both. We choreographed "Parallel Lines" in her garage over winter break, arguing about tempo and spacing and whether the audience would understand that when I knelt and she rose in her chair, we were the same height on purpose. She said they'd get it. I said we should add a spotlight cue. She said spotlights are a crutch for bad choreography. I didn't point out the irony. She already knew.

The technical problems were real. My teacher, Ms. Andrade, had never staged a piece with a wheelchair dancer and didn't know how to score the movement. A grand jeté covers about six feet of stage in one leap. Maya's chair covers the same distance in a roll, but the timing is different, the sight lines are different, and the floor has to be marked so her wheels don't catch on the seams in the marley. We spent two rehearsals just mapping her pathways across the stage, measuring turning radii, figuring out which formations worked when one dancer is at five feet and another is at three.

The piece ran four minutes and twelve seconds. In the final sequence, I danced behind Maya, matching her arm movements exactly while she led from the chair. The audience assumed I was leading. Maya's mother, sitting in the third row, knew better. After the show, she found me backstage and said, "You let her lead." I said, "She always was. I just stopped pretending otherwise."`,
    counselorNote: "The 'spotlights are a crutch for bad choreography' line — Maya's humor — is the essay's signature detail. The turning-radius and marley-floor specifics show this is a real artistic collaboration, not an inspirational anecdote. The final exchange with Maya's mother is devastating in its simplicity."
